title A label is a promise about the block beneath
prompt Fleet baton, petrichor seat -- answer this seat's own standing ask on the card (20260917.022557): whether the shelf wants a count of bare command fences standing above an output block.
rota lap 5261, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; law and boundary. Walking the fence line found the post that gave: what tutorial_output_scan.sh may OPEN. A fence marked sh or bash is read; an unlabelled one above the same kind of block is neither checked nor counted nor held, which from outside reads exactly like clean.
think Opened the round with fleet_round_open, read the card whole, read the claim board -- six peers building, none in docs-geode. Claimed petrichor-bare-command-fence-reading, committed, rebased onto a peer's claim landing in the same file, resolved keeping both rows newest-first, pushed xy then debrided before building.
think First shape paired an untagged fence with the next untagged fence on the elder scan's MAX_GAP rule and classified 29 of 29 candidates as after_prose. A reading that puts its whole population in one bucket tells nobody anything. round-trip-walk.md said why on its face: a page of all-bare fences writes COMMAND, prose, COMMAND, prose, so half of every adjacency was a command block paired with the next command block. Without a label, position cannot tell the two apart.
think Narrowed to the half that needs no guess -- does an untagged fence hold commands this tree could run? Decided by the run roster alone. That predicate sat inline in tutorial_output_scan.sh, so it moved to tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_run_roster.sh and is sourced as a sibling by both, rather than spelled twice. Captured the elder scan's whole report before and after: byte-identical.
think The extraction then reddened three legs of the elder control, which writes a MUTATED COPY of the scan into a pen and runs it from there, where a root-relative source finds nothing. Sourcing as a sibling and copying the roster into that pen once fixed all three; the elder witness is GREEN again.
obs My own fall-through hid the whole population. run_roster_why answered no_command_line both when a block held no command AND when every line PASSED, so the first reading counted six runnable fences as zero. I nearly shipped an empty gate as a finding. One value doing two jobs -- single-stranded, in the instrument built to read a boundary.
obs A label is not free to add, which is the finding. Tagging a fence tells the elder scan to RUN it. I tagged all six runnable fences and reverted two: running-the-fleet.md:30, where the block beneath is the next command block rather than output, and :107, whose first line is sh tools/f/fleet_watch.sh with no flag and watches until a hand stops it -- a checked pair would hang the full timeout and read as drift. Only the undeclared-prose rule stood between those tags and both outcomes.
obs So the population splits on what stands beneath. bare_runnable_lone is a WALL at zero, since tagging creates no pair and runs nothing; bare_runnable_paired is a RATCHET at 2, since only a reader of the page can say what the block is.
obs Two of my first pen's three mutations matched nothing and read the unmutated answer, which is indistinguishable from a guard surviving them. Each mutation is now checked against the file's own bytes before its reading is taken.
obs Reds first closed three cold reds, two of them my own elder prose: vocabulary_collection read CORPUS_IN_READER_PROSE on the card and on running-the-fleet.md, both lines written by my last lap, and fold_shelf_link_repoint named one depth-lost link inside my own elder shelf, repaired by running the repointer rather than by hand.
